                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mi Cat
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    [C,D] \;\in\; Cat
            %p
              The notation alludes to the fact that this makes
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cat"} Cat
              a
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/closed+monoidal+category"} closed monoidal category
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Cat
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Cat
              is in fact a
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cartesian+closed+category"} cartesian closed category
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi D
                        %mi C
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D^C
              = succeed ".)" do
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/exponential+objects"} exponential objects
            %p
              In fact, if we want
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Cat
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Cat
              to be
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cartesian+closed+category"} cartesian closed
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/adjoint+functor"} adjoint functor
              is unique). This is discussed in
              = succeed "." do
                %a{:href => "#InTermsOfCartMon"} a section below
            %p
              There is also a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/horizontal+composition"} horizontal composition
              of natural transformations, which makes
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cat"} Cat
              a
              = succeed ":" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/2-category"} 2-category
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Godement+product"} Godement product
            %p
              In fact,
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cat"} Cat
              is a 2-category (a
              = succeed "-enriched" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Cat
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Cat
              %em because
              it is (cartesian) closed: closed monoidal categories are automatically enriched over themselves, via their
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/internal+hom"} internal hom
          %p
          #CategoryOfPresheaves.num_remark
            %h6 Remark
            %p
              %strong
                (
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/categories+of+presheaves"}> categories of presheaves
                )
              %br/
              The
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/functor+categories"} functor categories
              %a.maruku-eqref{:href => "#eq:FunctorCategory"} (1)
              are also called
              = succeed "," do
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/categories+of+presheaves"} categories of presheaves
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi PSh
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi C
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo ≔
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %msup
                      %mi C
                      %mi op
                    %mo ,
                    %mi Set
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo ,
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    PSh(C) \,\coloneqq\, [C^{op}, Set]
                    \\,,
            %p hence if they are categories whose
            %ul
              %li
                %p
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/objects"} objects
                  are
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/functors"} functors
                  out of the
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/opposite+category"} opposite category
                  of a given category
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi C
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
                  into the category
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Set"} Set
                  of
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sets"} sets
                  and
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/functions"} functions
              %li
                %p
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/morphisms"} morphisms
                  are natural transformations between these.
            %p Similarly, functor categories of the form
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi CPSh
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi C
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo ≔
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mi C
                    %mo ,
                    %mi Set
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    CPSh(C) \,\coloneqq\, [C, Set]
            %p
              are also called
              = succeed "." do
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/categories+of+copresheaves"} categories of copresheaves
            %p
          %h3#morphismwiseDefn In terms of morphismwise components
          %p
            An alternative but ultimately equivalent way to define a natural transformation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi α
                  %mo :
                  %mi F
                  %mo →
                  %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \alpha : F \rightarrow G
            is as an assignment to every morphism
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi m
                  %mo :
                  %mi x
                  %mo →
                  %mi y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} m : x \rightarrow y
            in
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            of a morphism
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi α
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi m
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo :
                    %mi F
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi x
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo →
                    %mi G
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi y
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \alpha(m) : F(x) \rightarrow G(y)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi G
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi m
                    %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mi α
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi m
                    %mn 0
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mi α
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi m
                    %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mi F
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi m
                    %mn 0
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G(m_1)\alpha(m_0) = \alpha(m_1)F(m_0)
            for every binary composition
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi m
                    %mn 1
                  %msub
                    %mi m
                    %mn 0
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} m_1 m_0
            in
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            (or equivalently
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi α
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi m
                    %mn 2
                  %msub
                    %mi m
                    %mn 1
                  %msub
                    %mi m
                    %mn 0
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mi G
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi m
                    %mn 2
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mi α
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi m
                    %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mi F
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi m
                    %mn 0
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \alpha(m_2 m_1 m_0) = G(m_2) \alpha(m_1) F(m_0)
            for every ternary composition
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi m
                    %mn 2
                  %msub
                    %mi m
                    %mn 1
                  %msub
                    %mi m
                    %mn 0
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} m_2m_1m_0
            in
            = succeed ")." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
          %p
            The relation of this to the previous definition is that the commutative squares in the previous definition for any morphism
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
            give the value
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi α
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi f
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %mi G
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi f
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ∘
                    %msub
                      %mi α
                      %mi x
                    %mo =
                    %msub
                      %mi α
                      %mi y
                    %mo ∘
                    %mi F
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi f
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \alpha(f) = G(f) \circ \alpha_x = \alpha_y \circ F(f)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
            give the component
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi α
                      %mi x
                    %mo =
                    %mi α
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi id
                      %mi x
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \alpha_x = \alpha(id_x)
          %p
            Vertical composition of natural transformations can be specified directly in terms of this account as well: specifically, an
            = succeed "-ary" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi α
                    %mn 1
                  %mo .
                  %mo .
                  %mo .
                  %msub
                    %mi α
                    %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \alpha_1 ... \alpha_n
            of natural transformations is uniquely determined by the property that
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi α
                      %mn 1
                    %mo .
                    %mo .
                    %mo .
                    %msub
                      %mi α
                      %mi n
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi m
                      %mn 1
                    %mo .
                    %mo .
                    %mo .
                    %msub
                      %mi m
                      %mi n
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %msub
                      %mi α
                      %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi m
                      %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo .
                    %mo .
                    %mo .
                    %msub
                      %mi α
                      %mi n
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi m
                      %mi n
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\alpha_1 ... \alpha_n)(m_1 ... m_n) = \alpha_1(m_1) ... \alpha_n(m_n)
            = succeed "-ary" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi m
                    %mn 1
                  %mo .
                  %mo .
                  %mo .
                  %msub
                    %mi m
                    %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} m_1 ... m_n
            in
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
          %p
            Horizontal composition is even easier, as the horizontal composite of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi α
                    %mn 1
                  %mo ,
                  %mo .
                  %mo .
                  %mo .
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mi α
                    %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \alpha_1, ..., \alpha_n
            is just
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi α
                      %mn 1
                    %mo .
                    %mo .
                    %mo .
                    %msub
                      %mi α
                      %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \alpha_1 ... \alpha_n
          %h3#InTermsOfCartMon
            In terms of the cartesian closed monoidal structure on
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Cat
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Cat
          %p
            The definition of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/functor+category"} functor category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi C
                  %mo ,
                  %mi D
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [C,D]
            with morphisms being natural transformations is precisely the one that makes
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Cat
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Cat
            a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cartesian+monoidal+category"} cartesian
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/closed+monoidal+category"} closed monoidal category
          %p
            The category
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cat"} Cat
            of all categories (regarded for the moment just as an ordinary 1-
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category"}> category
            ) is a
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cartesian+monoidal+category"} cartesian monoidal category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi D
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D
            there is the cartesian
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/product"} product
            category
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                    %mo ×
                    %mi D
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C \times D
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            and
            = succeed ":" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi D
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Mor
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi C
                    %mo ×
                    %mi D
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %mi Mor
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi C
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ×
                    %mi Mor
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi D
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Mor(C \times D) = Mor(C) \times Mor(D)
          %p
            It therefore makes sense to ask if there is for each category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi Cat
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C \in Cat
            an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/internal+hom"} internal hom
            functor
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi C
                  %mo ,
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %mo :
                  %mi Cat
                  %mo →
                  %mi Cat
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [C,-] : Cat \to Cat
            that would make
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cat"} Cat
            into a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/closed+monoidal+category"} closed monoidal category
            in that for
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                  %mo ,
                  %mi B
                  %mo ,
                  %mi C
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi Cat
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A,B,C \in Cat
            we have natural isomorphisms of
            %em sets
            of functors
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Funct
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi A
                  %mo ×
                  %mi C
                  %mo ,
                  %mi B
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ≃
                  %mi Funct
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi A
                  %mo ,
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi C
                  %mo ,
                  %mi B
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  Funct(A \times C , B) \simeq Funct(A, [C,B])
                  \\,.
          %p
            This is precisely the case for
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi C
                  %mo ,
                  %mi B
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [C,B]
            being the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/functor+category"} functor category
            with functors
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                  %mo →
                  %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C \to B
            as objects and natural transformations, as defined above, as morphisms.
          %p
            Since
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Cat
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Cat
            here is cartesian closed, one often uses the exponential notation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi C
                    %mi B
                  %mo :
                  %mo =
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi B
                  %mo ,
                  %mi C
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C^B := [B,C]
            for the functor category.
          %p
            To derive from this the definition of natural transformations above, it is sufficient to consider the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/interval+category"} interval category
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                    %mo :
                    %mo =
                    %mi I
                    %mo :
                    %mo =
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                    %mi a
                    %mo →
                    %mi b
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A := I := \{a \to b\}
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi E
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi I
                  %mo →
                  %mi E
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} I \to E
            is precisely a choice of morphism in
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi E
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/internal+hom"} internal hom
            category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi C
                  %mo ,
                  %mi B
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [C,B]
            is by checking what functors
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi I
                  %mo →
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi C
                  %mo ,
                  %mi B
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} I \to [C,B]
            are. But by the defining property of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi C
                  %mo ,
                  %mi B
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [C,B]
            as an
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/internal+hom"} internal hom
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi I
                    %mo ×
                    %mi C
                    %mo →
                    %mi B
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} I \times C \to B
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Funct
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi I
                  %mo ,
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi C
                  %mo ,
                  %mi B
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ≃
                  %mi Funct
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi I
                  %mo ×
                  %mi C
                  %mo ,
                  %mi B
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  Funct(I, [C,B]) \simeq Funct(I \times C, B)
                  \\,.
          %p
            But, as mentioned above, we know what the category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi I
                  %mo ×
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} I \times C
            is like: its morphisms are pairs of morphisms in
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi I
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} I
            and
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                  %mo :
                  %msub
                    %mi c
                    %mn 1
                  %mo →
                  %msub
                    %mi c
                    %mn 2
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f : c_1 \to c_2
            any morphism in
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            we have
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mtable{:columnalign => "right left right left right left right left right left", :columnspacing => "0em", :displaystyle => "true"}
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %msub
                            %mi c
                            %mn 1
                          %mo ,
                          %mi a
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mover
                            %mo →
                            %mrow
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                              %mi f
                              %mo ,
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                              %mi a
                              %mo →
                              %mi b
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %msub
                            %mi c
                            %mn 2
                          %mo ,
                          %mi b
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mtd
                          %mo =
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %msub
                            %mi c
                            %mn 1
                          %mo ,
                          %mi a
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mover
                            %mo →
                            %mrow
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                              %mi f
                              %mo ,
                              %mi Id
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %msub
                            %mi c
                            %mn 2
                          %mo ,
                          %mi a
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mover
                            %mo →
                            %mrow
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                              %mi Id
                              %mo ,
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                              %mi a
                              %mo →
                              %mi b
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %msub
                            %mi c
                            %mn 2
                          %mo ,
                          %mi b
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mo =
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %msub
                            %mi c
                            %mn 1
                          %mo ,
                          %mi a
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mover
                            %mo →
                            %mrow
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                              %mi Id
                              %mo ,
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                              %mi a
                              %mo →
                              %mi b
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %msub
                            %mi c
                            %mn 1
                          %mo ,
                          %mi b
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mover
                            %mo →
                            %mrow
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                              %mi f
                              %mo ,
                              %mi Id
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %msub
                            %mi c
                            %mn 2
                          %mo ,
                          %mi b
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\begin{aligned}
                  (c_1,a) \stackrel{(f,(a \to b))}{\to} (c_2,b)
                  \&amp; =
                  (c_1,a) \stackrel{(f, Id)}{\to} (c_2,a)
                  \\stackrel{(Id, (a \to b)}{\to}
                  (c_2, b)
                  \\\
                  \&amp;=
                  (c_1,a) \stackrel{(Id, (a\to b))}{\to} (c_1,b)
                  \\stackrel{(f,Id)}{\to}
                  (c_2, b)
                  \\end{aligned}
          %p Here the right side is more conveniently depicted as a commuting square
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mtable
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %msub
                            %mi c
                            %mn 1
                          %mo ,
                          %mi a
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mtd
                          %mover
                            %mo →
                            %mrow
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                              %mi f
                              %mo ,
                              %mi Id
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mtd
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %msub
                            %mi c
                            %mn 2
                          %mo ,
                          %mi a
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %msup
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                            %mpadded{:width => "0"}
                              %mrow
                                %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                                %mi Id
                                %mo ,
                                %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                                %mi a
                                %mo →
                                %mi b
                                %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                                %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %msup
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                            %mpadded{:width => "0"}
                              %mrow
                                %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                                %mi Id
                                %mo ,
                                %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                                %mi a
                                %mo →
                                %mi b
                                %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                                %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %msub
                            %mi c
                            %mn 1
                          %mo ,
                          %mi b
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mtd
                          %mover
                            %mo →
                            %mrow
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                              %mi f
                              %mo ,
                              %mi Id
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mtd
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
