                  \\,.
          %h3#in_terms_of_double_categories In terms of double categories
          %p
            There is a nice way of describing these structures due to
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Charles+Ehresmann"} Charles Ehresmann
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi D
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D
            let
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo □
                  %mi D
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mo ∘
                    %mn 1
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mo ∘
                    %mn 2
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\square D,\circ_1,\circ_2)
            be the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/double+category"} double category
            of commutative squares in
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi D
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                  %mo →
                  %mi D
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C \to D
            can be described as
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Cat
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi C
                    %mo ,
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mo □
                    %mi D
                    %mo ,
                    %msub
                      %mo ∘
                      %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Cat(C,(\square D,\circ_1))
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mo ∘
                    %mn 2
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \circ_2
            induces a category structure on this and so we get
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi CAT
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi C
                    %mo ,
                    %mi D
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} CAT(C,D)
          %p An advantage of this approach is that it applies to the case of topological categories and groupoids (working in a convenient category of spaces).
          %p
            An analogous approach works for strict cubical
            = succeed "-categories" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ω
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \omega
            = succeed "s," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cube"} cube
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/closed+monoidal+category"} monoidal closed structure
            for these objects. This yields by an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equivalence+of+categories"} equivalence of categories
            a monoidal closed structure on
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/strict+omega-category"} strict globular omega-categories
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tensor+product"} tensor product
            is the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Crans-Gray+tensor+product"} Crans-Gray tensor product
          %h2#Examples Examples
