              to the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/composition"} composition
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:maxsize => "1.2em", :minsize => "1.2em"} (
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %msup
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo *
                    %msup
                      %mo{:maxsize => "1.2em", :minsize => "1.2em"} )
                      %mo *
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \big((-)^{\ast}\big)^\ast
              of the
              = succeed "-" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/linear+dual+space"} linear dual
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/endofunctor"} endofunctor
              with itself.
            %p
              This example was the motivating example of
              %a{:href => "#EilenbergMacLane45"} Eilenberg &amp; MacLane 1945
              (right on the first pages) for introducing the notion of natural transformation (and with it
              = succeed ")" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+theory"} category theory
            %p
              The conceptual subtlety that these authors sought to resolve here is that for any
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/finite-dimensional+vector+space"} finite-dimensional
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/vector+space"} vector space
              there exists also an
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/isomorphism"} isomorphism
              from
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi V
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
              to its
              = succeed "-" do
                %em single
              = succeed ":" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dual+vector+space"} dual vector space
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi V
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi 𝕂
                    %msup
                      %mi Vect
                      %mi fdim
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mo ⊢
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mo ∃
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mi V
                    %mover
                      %mo →
                      %mo ∼
                    %msup
                      %mi V
                      %mo *
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    V \in \mathbb{K} Vect^{fdim}
                    \\;\;\;\;\;\;
                    \\vdash
                    \\;\;\;\;\;\;
                    \\exists
                    \\;
                    V \xrightarrow{\sim} V^\ast
                    \\,.
            %p
              But these linear maps are conceptually different from
              %a.maruku-eqref{:href => "#eq:UnitMapToDoubleDual"} (2)
              in that they are
              %em not natural
              in the technical sense that they do not form the components of a natural transformation between the evident functors. Instead they involve an arbitrary choice equivalent to that of an (possibly indefinite)
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/inner+product"} inner product
              on
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi V
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/linear+isomorphisms"} linear isomorphisms
              (but just by the corresponding
              = succeed ")." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/isometries"} isometries
          %p
          .num_remark
            %h6 Example
            %p
              The
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/determinant"} determinant
              is a natural transformation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi det
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %msub
                      %mi GL
                      %mi n
                    %mo →
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %msup
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ×
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} det\colon GL_n\rightarrow (-)^\times
              from the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/general+linear+group"} general linear group
              to the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/group+of+units"} group of units
              of a
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ring"} ring
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Ring"} Ring
              to
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Grp"} Grp
          %p
          .num_remark
            %h6 Example
            %p
              The
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Frobenius+homomorphism"} Frobenius homomorphism
              is a natural transformation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi Frob
                      %mi p
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %msub
                      %mi Id
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi Ring
                          %mi p
                    %mo ⇒
                    %msub
                      %mi Id
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi Ring
                          %mi p
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Frob_p\colon Id_{Ring_p}\Rightarrow Id_{Ring_p}
              from the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/identity+functor"} identity functor
              on the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/full+subcategory"} full subcategory
              of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Ring"} Ring
              containing all rings with
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/characteristic"} characteristic
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi p
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p
              to itself.
          %p
          .num_remark
            %h6 Example
            %p
              The
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hurewicz+homomorphism"} Hurewicz homomorphism
              is a natural transformation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi h
                      %mi n
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %msub
                      %mi π
                      %mi n
                    %mo ⇒
                    %msub
                      %mi H
                      %mi n
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %mo ;
                    %mi ℤ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} h_n\colon\pi_n\Rightarrow H_n(-;\mathbb{Z})
              from the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+group"} homotopy group
              to
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/singular+homology"} singular homology
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Top"} Top
              to
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Grp"} Grp
          %p
          .num_remark
            %h6 Example
            %p
              The
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/inverse+element"} inversion
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi G
                    %mo →
                    %msup
                      %mi G
                      %mi op
                    %mo ,
                    %mi g
                    %mo ↦
                    %msup
                      %mi g
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                        %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G \rightarrow G^{op},g\mapsto g^{-1}
              for every
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/group"} group
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi G
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
              yields a natural transformation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mo{:lspace => "0em", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} Id
                      %mi Grp
                    %mo ⇒
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %msup
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mi op
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Id_{Grp}\Rightarrow (-)^{op}
              from the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/identity+functor"} identity functor
              on
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Grp"} Grp
              to the
              = succeed "-assigning" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/opposite+group"} opposite group
          %p
          .num_remark
            %h6 Example
            %p
              The
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coprojection"} coprojection
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi G
                    %mo →
                    %msup
                      %mi G
                      %mi ab
                    %mo ,
                    %mi g
                    %mo ↦
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mi g
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G\rightarrow G^{ab},g\mapsto[g]
              for every group
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi G
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
              yields a natural transformation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mo{:lspace => "0em", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} Id
                      %mi Grp
                    %mo ⇒
                    %msup
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                      %mi ab
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Id_{Grp}\Rightarrow -^{ab}
              from the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/identity+functor"} identity functor
              on
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Grp
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Grp
              to the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/abelianization"} abelianization
              functor.
          %p
          #HomomorphismsOfDiagrams.num_remark
            %h6 Example
            %p
              %strong (homomorphisms of diagrams)
              %br/
              By Remark
              = succeed "," do
                %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#CategoryOfPresheaves"}
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+of+presheaves"} category of presheaves
              or
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/copresheaves"} copresheaves
              has its morphisms identified with natural transformations.
            %p
              For instance, the category of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/directed+graphs"} directed graphs
              (
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/digraphs"}> digraphs
              ) may be identified with the category of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/copresheaves"} copresheaves
              on the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/diagram"} diagram
              shape
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:maxsize => "1.2em", :minsize => "1.2em"} {
                      %mi V
                      %mo ⇉
                      %mi E
                      %mo{:maxsize => "1.2em", :minsize => "1.2em"} }
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \big\{ V \rightrightarrows E\big\}
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:maxsize => "1.2em", :minsize => "1.2em"} {
                    %mi V
                    %mo ⇉
                    %mi E
                    %mo{:maxsize => "1.2em", :minsize => "1.2em"} }
                    %mo ⟶
                    %mi Set
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \big\{ V \rightrightarrows E \big\} \longrightarrow Set
              are identified with
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/digraph"} digraph
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homomorphisms"} homomorphisms
          %h2#variations Variations
          %p
            For functors between
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/n-category"} higher categories
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/lax+natural+transformation"} lax natural transformation
            etc.
          %p
            A transformation which is natural only relative to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/isomorphisms"} isomorphisms
            may be called a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/core-natural+transformation"} canonical transformation
          %p
            For functors with more complicated shapes than
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                    %mo ⇉
                    %mi D
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C \rightrightarrows D
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/extranatural+transformation"} extranatural transformation
            and
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dinatural+transformation"} dinatural transformation
          %h2#related_entries Related entries
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/intertwiner"} intertwiner
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy"} homotopy
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/transfor"} transfor
              %ul
                %li
                  %p
                    %strong natural transformation
                  %ul
                    %li
                      %p
                        %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/transformation+of+adjoints"} transformation of adjoints
                    %li
                      %p
                        %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monad+transformation"} monad transformation
                    %li
                      %p
                        %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/extranatural+transformation"}> extranatural transformation
                        ,
                        %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dinatural+transformation"} dinatural transformation
                    %li
                      %p
                        %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cartesian+natural+transformation"} cartesian natural transformation
                    %li
                      %p
                        %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/enriched+natural+transformation"} enriched natural transformation
                    %li
                      %p
                        %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/strong+natural+transformation"} strong natural transformation
                %li
                  %p
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pseudonatural+transformation"} pseudonatural transformation
                %li
                  %p
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/lax+natural+transformation"} lax natural transformation
            %li
              %p
                See
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/natural+transformation+%28discussion%29"} natural transformation (discussion)
                for an informal discussion about natural transformations.
          %h2#references References
          %p
            The notion of
            %em natural transformations
            between functors is due to
          %ul
            %li#EilenbergMacLane45
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Samuel+Eilenberg"}> Samuel Eilenberg
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Saunders+MacLane"} Saunders MacLane
              = succeed "," do
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/General+Theory+of+Natural+Equivalences"} General Theory of Natural Equivalences
              %strong 58
              2 (1945) 231-294 &amp;lbrack;
              %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1090/S0002-9947-1945-0013131-6"}> doi:10.1090/S0002-9947-1945-0013131-6
              ,
              = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                %a{:href => "http://www.jstor.org/stable/1990284"} jstor:1990284
          %p
            where it served as the motivation for the definition of
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/categories"} categories
            and
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/functors"} functors
            in the first place.
          %p Textbook accounts:
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Saunders+MacLane"}> Saunders MacLane
                , §I.4 of:
                = succeed "," do
                  %em
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Categories+for+the+Working+Mathematician"} Categories for the Working Mathematician
                %strong 5
                Springer (1971, second ed. 1997) &amp;lbrack;
                %a{:href => "https://link.springer.com/book/10.1007/978-1-4757-4721-8"}> doi:10.1007/978-1-4757-4721-8
                \&amp;rbrack;
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Francis+Borceux"}> Francis Borceux
                , Section 1.3 in:
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Handbook+of+Categorical+Algebra"} Handbook of Categorical Algebra
                Vol. 1:
                %em Basic Category Theory
                \&amp;lbrack;
                %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1017/CBO9780511525858"}> doi:10.1017/CBO9780511525858
                \&amp;rbrack;
          %p
            See also
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+theory#references"} category theory - references
